"S´eminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire 91 (2025), Article B91b (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/wpapers/s91alia.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; THE WEAK ACYCLIC MATCHING PROPERTY IN ABELIAN GROUPS ... Following [2], we deﬁne an abelian group G to possess the weak acyclic matching ... answer to Conjecture 1.5 and thoroughly addresses Question 1.6. ... paper is related to the classical theory introduced by Philip Hall in 1935, the two\nTHE WEAK ACYCLIC MATCHING PROPERTY IN ABELIAN GROUPS\n3\nFollowing [2], we deﬁne an abelian group G to possess the weak acyclic matching\nproperty if, for every ﬁnite nonempty subsets A and B of G with |A| = |B| and\nA ∩(A + B) = ∅, A is acyclically matched to B. In [2], the following conjecture\nconcerning the weak acyclic matching property was proposed.\nConjecture 1.5. There are inﬁnitely many n for which Z/nZ has the weak acyclic\nmatching property.\nThe following question was also raised in [1].\nQuestion 1.6. Does Z/pZ possess the weak acyclic matching property for every\nprime p?\nIn this note, our main result, stated as Theorem 2.3, provides an aﬃrmative\nanswer to Conjecture 1.5 and thoroughly addresses Question 1.6.\nFurthermore,\nTheorem 2.3 classiﬁes all abelian groups with respect to their adherence to the weak\nacyclic matching property.\nRemark 1.7. It is worth noting that while the matching theory developed in this\npaper is related to the classical theory introduced by Philip Hall in 1935, the two\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll works · Hexagon (https://hexagonmath.org/search?cursor=eyJ2IjoxLCJzb3J0RGF0ZSI6IjIwMjYtMTAtMjFUMTQ6MjI6MTEuMjQyWiIsImlkZW50aWZpZXIiOiIyNjEwLjAwNDM4In0&direction=next&page=3&per_page=100)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Primarily AI-generated text Human understanding: no parts Hurwitz stability Lean formalization acyclic polynomial counterexample graph polynomial ... ### Counterexamples to the multicolor nonnested matching conjecture ... We show that excluding nested pairs can increase the number of vertices needed to force a matching of one color, disproving a conjecture of Barát, Gyárfás and Tóth.\n\nhexagon:2610.00325v1\n\nmath.CO — Combinatorics\n\n### A Nonunimodal Acyclic Polynomial with All Roots in the Left Half-Plane\n\nContributed by YURAN XIU\n\nLet $A(G,x)$A(G,x) count vertex subsets of a graph $G$G that induce forests, by their cardinalities. Barton, Brown, and Pike asked whether failure of coefficient unimodality forces $A(G,x)$A(G,x) to have a root with positive real part. We give a negative answer. The join of $K_{46}$K 46​ with three disjoint copies of $P_3$P 3​ has a nonunimodal acyclic polynomial of degree nine, yet all its complex roots have strictly negative real part. We derive the polynomial by classifying induced forests and give its exact positive Hurwitz determinants. A Lean formalization verifies the graph-to-polynomial identity and the full counterexample, using an exact rational Lyapunov certificate for strict stability. HAL deposit: hal-05800220v1.\n\nPrimarily AI-generated text Human understanding: no parts Hurwitz stability Lean formalization acyclic polynomial counterexample graph polynomial\n\nOct 18, 2026 4 pages\n\nPDFView work\n\nhexagon:2610.00326v1\n\nmath.CO — Combinatorics\n\n### A Bipartite Graph with a Nonunimodal Acyclic Polynomial\n\nContributed by YURAN XIU\n\nWe construct a bipartite graph on 230 vertices whose acyclic polynomial, which enumerates vertex subsets inducing forests, is not unimodal. This answers Question 3.3 of Barton, Brown, and Pike negatively. The graph has a common set of 50 vertices and 15 branches, each with two sets of six vertices. A partition according to the number of selected common vertices gives a three-term formula for the complete induced-forest polynomial. Its coefficients have a strict descent from degree 62 to degree 63 and a later strict ascent from degree 66 to degree 67. The actual graph, the unrestricted subset-counting formula, and the exact inequalities have been checked in Lean. HAL deposit: hal-05800225v1.\n\nPrimarily AI-generated text Human understanding: no parts Lean formalization acyclic polynomial bipartite graphs counterexample unimodality\n\nOct 18, 2026 4 pages\n\nPDFView work\n\nhexagon:2610.00329v1\n\nmath.CO — Combinatorics\n\n### Counterexamples to the multicolor nonnested matching conjecture\n\nContributed by Pedro Machado Manhães de Castro\n\nWe show that excluding nested pairs can increase the number of vertices needed to force a matching of one color, disproving a conjecture of Barát, Gyárfás and Tóth. A matching consists of edges with no shared endpoint. In a fixed vertex order, two edges are nested if both endpoints of one lie between those of the other. For $r$r colors and a target of $k$k edges, the classical theorem guarantees a matching of one color on $(r-1)(k-1)+2k$(r−1)(k−1)+2 k vertices when every pair is joined by an edge. For every $r,k\\ge3$r,k≥3, we construct a coloring at this very threshold in which every such matching contains a nested pair. Thus the nonnested threshold is larger by at least one vertex. Already with three colors, ten vertices force three disjoint edges of one color, while our coloring makes every such triple contain a nested pair. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe weak acyclic matching property in abelian groups (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2404.02178)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The objective of this note is to explore a related concept, known as the weak acyclic matching property, within the context of abelian groups.\nSubmitted 06 Aug 2025\n\n# The weak acyclic matching property in abelian groups\n\nImageUniversity of California, San Diego\n\nMA\n\nMohsen Aliabadi\n\nPT\n\nPeter Taylor\n\n## Abstract\n\nA matching from a finite subset $A\\subset\\mathbb{Z}^n$A⊂Z n to another subset $B\\subset\\mathbb{Z}^n$B⊂Z n is a bijection $f : A \\rightarrow B$f:A→B with the property that $a+f(a)$a+f(a) never lies in $A$A. A matching is called acyclic if it is uniquely determined by its multiplicity function. Alon et al. established the acyclic matching property for $\\mathbb{Z}^n$Z n, which was later extended to all abelian torsion-free groups. In a prior work, the authors of this paper settled the acyclic matching property for all abelian groups. The objective of this note is to explore a related concept, known as the weak acyclic matching property, within the context of abelian groups.\n\nView Paper\n\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{aliabadi2025weakacyclicmatchingproperty, title={The weak acyclic matching property in abelian groups}, author={Mohsen Aliabadi and Peter Taylor}, year={2025}, eprint={2404.02178}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02178}, }\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe weak acyclic matching property in abelian groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02178)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; The objective of this note is to explore a related concept, known as the weak acyclic matching property, within the context of abelian groups.\nTitle: The weak acyclic matching property in abelian groups\nAuthors: Mohsen Aliabadi, Peter Taylor\nDate: Mon Apr  1 21:29:17 2024\n\nA matching from a finite subset $A\\subset\\mathbb{Z}^n$ to another subset $B\\subset\\mathbb{Z}^n$ is a bijection $f : A \\rightarrow B$ with the property that $a+f(a)$ never lies in $A$. A matching is called acyclic if it is uniquely determined by its multiplicity function. Alon et al. established the acyclic matching property for $\\mathbb{Z}^n$, which was later extended to all abelian torsion-free groups. In a prior work, the authors of this paper settled the acyclic matching property for all abelian groups. The objective of this note is to explore a related concept, known as the weak acyclic matching property, within the context of abelian groups.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-05-27 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-05-27)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: today; The Abu-Khzam--Langston conjecture, that is the weak-immersion analogue of Hadwiger's conjecture and a weak version of an earlier conjecture of Lescure and Meyniel, asserts that every graph $G$ contains a weak immersion of $K_{χ(G)}$. ... Along the way, we introduce a notion of \\emph{cycle-matching colouring} of a graph, a relaxation of edge-colouring in which colour classes induce vertex-disjoint unions of edges and odd cycles, and prove a sharpening of Vizing's theorem in this setting: every multigraph admits a cycle-matching colouring with at most $Δ(G)$ colours. ... We disprove the conjecture by proving that $Q_{14h-1}(x)$ and $Q_{30h-1}(x)$ have repeated roots for every $h\\ge1$; in particular, $k=13$ is the smallest counterexample.\n\n2026-05-27\n\nJonathan C. Dahlke\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nThe Abu-Khzam$\\unicode{x2013}$Langston Conjecture for Graphs with $α(G) = 2$\n\nThe Abu-Khzam--Langston conjecture, that is the weak-immersion analogue of Hadwiger's conjecture and a weak version of an earlier conjecture of Lescure and Meyniel, asserts that every graph $G$ contains a weak immersion of $K_{χ(G)}$. We prove the conjecture for the class of graphs of independence number two. Along the way, we introduce a notion of \\emph{cycle-matching colouring} of a graph, a relaxation of edge-colouring in which colour classes induce vertex-disjoint unions of edges and odd cycles, and prove a sharpening of Vizing's theorem in this setting: every multigraph admits a cycle-matching colouring with at most $Δ(G)$ colours.\n\ncontain graph independence number coloring\n\n2026-05-27\n\nXuejun Guo, Zhengyu Tao\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nCounterexamples to Stanley's conjecture on dimer coverings\n\nLet $Q_k(x)$ be Stanley's explicit denominator for the dimer-covering generating function $F_k(x)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}A_{k,n}x^n$ of $k\\times n$ rectangles. Stanley conjectured in 1985 that $Q_k(x)$ has only simple roots; this longstanding conjecture was recently recorded in Lai's list of open problems on tilings (see [6, Problem 33]). We disprove the conjecture by proving that $Q_{14h-1}(x)$ and $Q_{30h-1}(x)$ have repeated roots for every $h\\ge1$; in particular, $k=13$ is the smallest counterexample. The construction comes from two exceptional multiplicative identities among trigonometric algebraic units. We further propose a conjecture concerning this class of trigonometric identities, which appears to be related to Robinson's problem on primitive Pell factors.\n\nstanley\n\n2026-05-27\n\nIrena Rusu\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Matchings in Groups (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2418922_On_Matchings_in_Groups)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; : The purpose of this note is to give a constructive proof of a conjecture in Fan and Losonczy (1996) concerning the existence of acyclic matchings. ... An acyclic matching is a matching f such that for any matching g such that m f = m g , we have f = g. ... The conjecure arises in the study of the problem, considered by Wakeford [2], of deciding which sets of monomials are removable from a generic homogeneous polynomial using a linear change of variables.\n: The purpose of this note is to give a constructive proof of a conjecture in Fan and Losonczy (1996) concerning the existence of acyclic matchings. The rst and third authors were supported in part by a USA-Israel BSF grant. The second author was supported in part by a National Science Foundation postdoctoral fellowship. 2 1. Main res