iminant 1 only; and the Fermat census of Jumagulov (2608.18134) and Saller's split polarisations on CM sixfolds are routes for F3' and F2.\n  * A sharper chain bound for the seed constant. Along a reflexive descent in dimension four the normalised volume |2Δ| − 3|Δ| + 2 falls strictly and the dual volume rises, and the volume depends on h_2, which the point counts do not see (`construct/volume_chain.py`). The longest chain in (points, dual points, volume) bounds every descent and is never longer than the pair chain. Running it needs |2Δ| for the whole Kreuzer–Skarke list, a device run.\n  * The learn-from-failure log has a run `barrier-atlas` with the four records of the study, each with the rule it changed. No result here closes anything; every label stays as it was.\n\nTheir 5,035 items (1,471 open and 2,437 solved Formal Conjectures statements among them) are indexed in the package and load into memory as literature, so the engine asks whether a target is already settled before it attacks it. Nothing fetched is executed. `THIRD_PARTY.md` lists every source, its licence and what was taken.\n\nThree of the repositories added on 21 October gave the engine a part each: the audit protocol and golden-output runner of E. O. Kahya's de Sitter audit, the go/no-go triage of MontrealAI's AGI-Alpha-Agent, and the figure critic after PaperBanana (Zhu et al., arXiv:2601.23265). Next-Concept Modeling and ARTEMIS were read and nothing was taken from them.\n\nEvery system, corpus and method the engine takes from is in one registry, `src/ebllm/bllm/data/sources.json`, with its reference, its status (refereed, preprint, software, unrefereed), what was taken, the module that implements it, what Anaxia adds and what was left out. `anaxia sources check` searches the DeepMind index together with two more: the 782 problem dossiers of OpenRefute (The Omega Institute), each a published conjecture with its kernel-checked resolution, and the 722 manuscripts of OpenAI's mathematics collectio